# acestream-suite's catalogue as an M3U playlist that carries the guide.
#
# Installed through scripts/crons-install.sh (this file is in its JOBS list; the host crontab is
# generated from it, never edited by hand).
#
# The link it keeps alive is http://192.168.1.245/varios/acestream-suite-guide.m3u, served by the same Caddy
# that already serves /srv/www/varios over plain HTTP.
#
# IT IS NOT THE LIST THE TV READS TODAY. The TV reads hls.m3u, kept by acestream-suite-playlist.cron,
# and it is left alone on purpose: that list's `tvg-id` is the AceStream content id of every stream
# and it is what Dispatcharr account 4 consumes, so rewriting it in place would re-map the live
# line-up. This job only ever writes its own file, so the owner can point a player or an account at
# the new list when the guide has earned it. A consumer that still asks for another file name is
# served through ACESTREAM_SUITE_COMPAT_ALIASES (see below), not by a name written into the script.
#
# Every fifteen minutes, offset from acestream-suite-playlist's own 5,20,35,50 so the two jobs never fetch
# at the same instant. What changes between runs is the catalogue (channels added or removed), not
# the streams or the guide: one JSON export, one mapping fetch, one file write. The script writes
# only when the content differs, so an unchanged playlist keeps its mtime.
#
# The script takes its own flock and logs to /var/log/acestream-suite-guide-playlist.log, so there is nothing
# to collect here: a run that overlaps the previous one says so in that log and exits.
#
# An alias is where a consumer still asks for another file name: the script copies the list under the
# names in ACESTREAM_SUITE_COMPAT_ALIASES (`new-file=other-file`, comma-separated) and writes nothing
# extra when it is unset. The value lives on the host, not in git, and the line below loads it with
# POSIX sh; the `[ -r ... ]` guard is what keeps the job working on a host without the file.
# The file must EXPORT its values (`export ACESTREAM_SUITE_COMPAT_ALIASES=...`, or `set -a` on
# its first line): a plain assignment in a sourced file is a shell variable and never reaches
# the binary, which is a child process.
SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/sbin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/usr/local/bin
7,22,37,52 * * * * root [ -r /etc/acestream-suite.env ] && . /etc/acestream-suite.env; /usr/local/bin/acestream-suite-guide-playlist >/dev/null 2>&1
